diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — "two segments can't reach their gateway" — is not present in the live network. A complete diagnostic sweep across all four phases found no traffic-affecting fault.

Phase 1 reachability: all 28 tested host pairs return 0% packet loss, including hosts on VLAN5 (pc1, pc2, dc_pc5) and VLAN6 (pc3, pc4) — the exact two segments named in the ticket. Direct gateway ping tests confirm: pc1 → 10.5.0.254 (br_v5 on sshswitch, VLAN5 gateway) 0% loss; pc3 → 10.6.0.254 (br_v6, VLAN6 gateway) 0% loss; dc_pc5 → 10.5.0.254 0% loss. Cross-subnet routing (e.g., pc1 → 172.16.30.14) also succeeds, confirming the full path through sshswitch and router17 is operational.

The infra_sweep found all 15 hosts properly addressed with default routes and no gateway/resolver divergence. OSPF is fully converged across all 9 routers with 17 routes each. No ACL rules drop traffic. No TC shaping. No resource pressure.

One structural artifact was noted: sshswitch's eth0 (downlink from switch4) is a member of br_v1 — the same bridge that carries the router17 uplink (bond0, 55.5.1.0/24) — rather than the now-empty br0. This may be a side effect of maintenance activity on switch4 or sshswitch. However, because switch4's native VLAN1 bridge has no connected hosts, no traffic crosses that bridge and no service is disrupted. VLAN5 and VLAN6 continue to flow correctly through their respective tagged subinterfaces.

Root cause: No fault exists. The user's complaint is unconfirmed by live evidence; all gateway and routing paths are fully operational.

Fix: No corrective action required. If desired for architectural cleanliness, restore sshswitch eth0 to br0 and remove it from br_v1 — this has no functional effect given the current host layout.
=== END ===